@lomiification Huh? What are you on about? Deepseeks's financials aren't anything out of the ordinary. It's  a 50-100 billion dollar company, profitable on inference but still on a overall net loss due to other costs such as training and chips. Currently subsidised by the Chinese Government, as is their usual strat, but they are more efficient than Anthropic or OpenAI due to sanctions so it evens out. It also saves up a lot by training their models on Anthropic/OpenAI's latest ones to save research costs.

Overall, as solid as an AI company can be. Will still probably lose a lot of value/profits as the number of AI-startups die off, but nothing super dramatic. I heard the AI-investments in China tanked a bit, not sure how it will affect them.

If OpenAi and Antrhopic claimed they would be 100 billion dollar companies...nobody would really have an issue with them. But they claim to be worth 1-2 trillion dollars.

@maximusreed9270 No he didn't haha. Basically said "I was wrong a few years ago because I expected them to act rationally" is a cop out moving of the goals posts.

The frontier labs have been able to monetize the models they sell to an ever-growing customer base, creating the fastest growing companies in history. Not only that, as Anthropic's S-1 demonstrated they're not in fact burning all this cash that Ed claimed and they have a positive cash flow picture and viable unit economics.

Even worse his claims that AI had hit a wall time and time again as part of the basis were flat out, wildly incorrect. With models that keep leapfrogging one another in all relevant metrics and applications. In no way is he making a concession
